"Places | National Library Reference Guides (https://reference.nlb.gov.sg/arts/visual-arts/artxstacks/places/)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://reference.nlb.gov.sg/arts/visual-arts/artxstacks/places/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 183\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: A Singapore Government Agency Website How to identify\nL3: \nL4: Official website links end with .gov.sg\nL5: \nL6: Government agencies communicate via .gov.sg websites (e.g. go.gov.sg/open). 【1†Trusted websites (opens in new tab)†www.gov.sg】\nL7: \nL8: Secure websites use HTTPS\nL9: \nL10: Look for a lock () or https:// as an added precaution. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\nL11: \nL12: Information\nL13: This website is in the midst of an information transfer, and some links may not work as intended. If assistance is required, please email us at ref@nlb.gov.sg\nL14: # Places\nL15: \nL16: Explore books, articles, maps, photographs, and recordings about the history of the Singapore River, Chinatown, and the former National Stadium, tied to the artworks on Level 11 of the library.\nL17: \nL18: Last updated on 13 July 2026\nL19: \nL20: On this page\nL21: \nL22:   * 【2†Art X Stacks - Places】\nL23:   * 【3†Books】\nL24:   * 【4†BiblioAsia articles】\nL25:   * 【5†Infopedia articles】\nL26:   * 【6†Spotlight: Map Collections】\nL27:   * 【7†Spotlight: Photographs】\nL28:   * 【8†Spotlight: Audio-visual Collections】\nL29:   * 【9†Spotlight: MusicSG】\nL30: ## Art X Stacks - Places\nL31: \nL32: In this section, we delve deeper into the history of the places portrayed in the artworks on Level 11 of the library. The following resources tell us more about the transformation of the Singapore River, the community memories associated with Chinatown, and bring us back in time to the opening of the former National Stadium in 1973. As we reflect on these stories, we can revisit the paintings and the physical sites with fresh perspectives.\nL33: ## Books\nL34: \nL35: 【10†Image: Book cover with the title \"The Singapore River: A Social History\" showing historic Singapore riverbanks and boats in greenish tint.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL36: \nL37: Dobbs, Stephen. 【11†The Singapore River: A Social History, 1819-2002 ↗ (opens in new tab)†eservice.nlb.gov.sg】. Singapore: NUS Press, 2016.\nL38: Blending social history, geography, economic history and urban studies, Dobbs sets out the history of the Singapore River and of the people who made it their home and workplace. This text should be of interest to anyone wishing to understand Singapore's many transformations during the past two centuries. Also available as an 【12†ebook. ↗ (opens in new tab)†catalogue.nlb.gov.sg】\nL39: 【13†Image: Cover with title \"Clean Rivers,\" showing photo of Singapore River with clean water and skyscrapers, library barcode at bottom.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL40: \nL41: 【14†Clean Rivers: Cleaning up of Singapore River and Kallang Basin ↗ (opens in new tab)†eservice.nlb.gov.sg】. Singapore: Ministry of the Environment, 1987.\nL42: Commemorating the completion of a 10-year clean-up of the Singapore River and Kallang Basin, this booklet gives an introduction of the project, including its objectives, action plan and results.\nL43: \nL44: 【15†Image: Cover of \"Singapore Lifeline\" book featuring two men working on a boat with text in English and Chinese.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL45: \nL46: Oral History Department. 【16†Singapore Lifeline: The River and Its People ↗ (opens in new tab)†eservice.nlb.gov.sg】. Singapore: Times Books International, 1986.\nL47: This is a publication on the history of Singapore River by the Oral History Department and the National Archives. It contains maps and photographs of the Singapore River’s transformation as a vital artery of trade and transportation.\nL48: \nL49: 【17†Image: Cover image with the text \"Singapore River Walk,\" showing tall skyscrapers and buildings by the water.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL50: \nL51: Ng, Marcus. 【18†Singapore River Walk ↗ (opens in new tab)†eservice.nlb.gov.sg】. Singapore: National Heritage Board, 2016.\nL52: Produced by the National Heritage Board (NHB), this guidebook provides a history of the Singapore River, with photographs and interviews relating memories of the river. A section of the guidebook focuses on the bridges across the Singapore River. A digital copy can also be downloaded from 【19†Roots.sg ↗ (opens in new tab)†www.roots.gov.sg】.\nL53: \nL54: 【20†Image: Cover of a book titled \"Rethinking Chinatown and Heritage Conservation in Singapore\" over an aerial photo of buildings.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL55: Kwok, Kian Woon, C.J. Wan-Ling Wee, and Karen Chia, eds. 【21†Rethinking Chinatown and Heritage Conservation in Singapore ↗ (opens in new tab)†eservice.nlb.gov.sg】. Singapore: Singapore Heritage Society, 2000.\nL56: \nL57: In 1998, the Singapore Tourism Board unveiled plans to revitalise Chinatown. Stemming from the announcement, the Singapore Heritage Society undertook a study of Chinatown’s heritage and history, focusing on the challenges and opportunities in the redevelopment of the heritage district.\nL58: 【22†Image: Cover of \"Chinatown Memories\" showing a painted storefront with blue shutters and various decorations.†isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg】\nL59: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChinatown offers tenants 50% rent relief in bid to revive tourism hotspot | Video - CNA (https://www.channelnewsasia.com/watch/chinatown-offers-tenants-50-rent-relief-bid-revive-tourism-hotspot-video-3341836)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/watch/chinatown-offers-tenants-50-rent-relief-bid-revive-tourism-hotspot-video-3341836\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1915\nL0: Skip to main content\nL1: \nL2: 【0† 】\nL3: